<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#papyrus">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Papyrus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Papyrus is a writing material made from the pith of the papyrus plant, which grew abundantly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley and delta. It was the principal medium for records and literature in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and documents such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harris-papyrus">Harris Papyrus</a> are key sources for the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Empire</a> made papyrus production a state monopoly, and papyrus documents remained in use under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/papyrus.jpg" alt="Papyrus" width="320" height="297" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hunefer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papiro_de_Hunefer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
